## Serving the Protocol (`--with-server`)

`cmd/{{.Name}}/main.go` serves the Harbor Protocol from your own binary,
carrying the compiled in-process tools `RegisterTools` (in `agent.go`)
wires. It is the external-serving sibling of `harbor serve`: the same
production posture (a JWKS-backed JWT verifier built from
`identity.jwks_url` / `identity.jwks_file`; no dev signer, no mock),
reached through the public `github.com/hurtener/Harbor/sdk/server`
facade. Every tool `RegisterTools` registers keeps its declared
approval / OAuth / policy wrapping from `tools.entries` in `harbor.yaml`.

Because the server verifies every request against your identity
provider's JWK Set, the local-development loop is the three-command
`harbor token` flow:

```sh
# 1. Generate a signing keypair + public JWK Set.
harbor token keygen --out ./keys

# 2. In harbor.yaml, point identity.jwks_file at the emitted set
#    (and remove identity.jwks_url — set exactly one source):
#      identity:
#        jwks_file: ./keys/jwks.json

# 3. Build + run the server.
go build ./cmd/{{.Name}} && ./{{.Name}} --config ./harbor.yaml

# 4. Mint a short-lived JWT whose iss/aud match harbor.yaml and call
#    the Protocol with it (Authorization: Bearer <token>):
harbor token mint --key ./keys/private.pem \
  --tenant acme --user alice --session s1 \
  --issuer https://issuer.example.com --audience {{.Name}}
```

Before any non-local deployment, replace `identity.issuer` /
`identity.audience` / the JWKS source with your real OIDC provider's
values. The server binds `server.bind_addr` from `harbor.yaml`;
override it with `--bind <host:port>` or the `HARBOR_BIND` environment
variable (the flagless equivalent, mirroring `harbor serve`).
